import Applied.Statistics.GroupSequentialCanonical

/-!
# Group-sequential designs: canonical joint distribution of interim statistics

- id: stat-group-sequential-canonical
- area: statistics
- status: formalized
- source: C. Jennison, B. W. Turnbull, "Group Sequential Methods with Applications to Clinical Trials", Chapman & Hall/CRC (2000), the "canonical joint distribution" of Chapter 3 (section and equation numbers to be confirmed)
- used-in: computation of group-sequential stopping boundaries (Pocock, O'Brien–Fleming, alpha-spending) for interim analyses
- prerequisites: Mathlib's real Gaussian distribution (sums of independent Gaussians), covariance of sums, independence of σ-algebras generated by disjoint blocks
- statement-review: unreviewed

Informal statement. Let `P` be a probability measure and `ξ 0, ξ 1, …` real random variables.
Put `S n = ξ 0 + … + ξ (n-1)` and `Z n = S n / √n`. The information times are natural numbers
(numbers of unit-variance observations).

(1) Second-order structure, without normality. If the `ξ i` are square-integrable, pairwise
independent and each has variance one, then `Cov(S a, S b) = min(a, b)` for all `a, b`, and
`Cov(Z a, Z b) = √(a/b)` for `0 < a ≤ b`.

(2) Independent blocks. If the `ξ i` (indexed by any type) are measurable and mutually
independent, then their sums over pairwise disjoint finite sets of indices are mutually
independent. More generally, for mutually independent σ-algebras the σ-algebras generated by
pairwise disjoint blocks are mutually independent.

(3) Normal increments. If the `ξ i` are measurable, mutually independent and each has the
standard normal law, then `S n` has law `N(0, n)`; for `a ≤ b` the increment `S b - S a` has
law `N(0, b - a)`; for `n > 0` the statistic `Z n` has law `N(0, 1)`; and
`Cov(Z a, Z b) = √(a/b)` for `0 < a ≤ b`.

(4) Independent increments. If the `ξ i` are measurable and mutually independent (normality
is not used), then for every non-decreasing sequence of look times `t 0 ≤ t 1 ≤ …` the
increments `S (t (k+1)) - S (t k)`, `k = 0, 1, …`, are mutually independent. With `t 0 = 0`
the first of these is `S (t 1)`.

Conventions and differences from textbook statements. `N(0, 0)` is the point mass at zero
(Mathlib's `gaussianReal 0 0`), which is what (3) gives for `S 0` and for `a = b`. The drift
is zero: this is the null distribution. Looks are indexed by `ℕ`; a design with `K` looks is
obtained by restricting to the first `K` indices. Look times are only required to be
non-decreasing, so repeated looks give increments equal to zero.

Not covered here: the statement that the vector `(Z n₁, …, Z n_K)` is multivariate normal as a
law on `ℝ^K`, and that its law depends only on the look times (both in
`Targets/Statistics/GroupSequentialJointLaw.lean`); existence of boundaries attaining a given
alpha-spending function exactly (in `Targets/Statistics/GroupSequentialBoundaries.lean`; see
`Targets/Statistics/GroupSequential.lean` for the spending principle itself). Not covered
anywhere: non-zero drift `E[Z n] = θ √n`; information levels that are not integers; the
recursive numerical integration that yields boundaries.
-/

